 "naive_both_degenerates_at_concurrency": {
  "finding": "The straw man does not do badly at concurrency; it does nothing.",
  "detail": "`naive_both` is Proportional admission over both tensor classes -- 'what you get by turning two independent policies on at once' -- and Proportional answers a budget shortfall by shaving the requested hit ratio for every candidate alike. At four concurrent requests the concurrency trace offers 512 candidates against a 47 MB budget, so every shaved ratio falls under `min_hit_ratio` (0.05) and all 512 are declined: an EMPTY plan, 0 actions, indistinguishable on the device from the hook with no policy. On the recorded batch-1 trace the same preset emits 256 actions, so this is a property of concurrency and not of the preset.\n\nTwo consequences. First, the specification's straw man cannot demonstrate what it is for at the concurrencies the frontier actually scores -- it is supposed to show that unarbitrated persistence HURTS, and instead it abstains. Second, `eval/real_eval.py` refused it as a NULL CANDIDATE, which would have taken the other four arms of the five-arm run down with it; the guard now tells a planner that declined every candidate for a stated reason apart from a window that was computed and handed back to a runtime that never attached it."
 }
